"Three seasons of 60s frontier television." You just described Bonanza. TOS was something else.
The City on the Edge of Forever — Kirk lets Edith Keeler die to save history. He knows what he's doing when he does it. That's not frontier adventure, that's a moral cost paid in full, no reset button, no speech to soften it.
Let That Be Your Last Battlefield — two men, one black on the left, one black on the right, and their entire civilization destroyed over which side was superior. Aired in January 1969. Ask yourself how many episodes of Bonanza ended with the heroes watching a species annihilate itself and then quietly noting that the hatred had always been pointless.
A Taste of Armageddon — a planet runs a sanitized war on computers, killing its own citizens on schedule to keep the numbers clean. Kirk breaks the machine and forces them to face what war actually is. Aired 1967, during Vietnam.
The Omega Glory — Kirk recites the Constitution to a culture that has forgotten what it means. Sound like TNG? It was TNG, thirty years early, with a smaller budget and no safety net.
You're treating "episodic" as a weakness. It wasn't. It was the only format the network would let them have, and they used it to ask questions the network would never have approved if they'd been presented as a coherent philosophy. TNG could be a philosophy because TOS had spent three seasons proving that philosophy was watchable.
